Instead of repeatedly changing the input copy, this version starts with the largest place value and moves it down by powers of ten. Each extracted digit is checked for odd parity.

For `n = 86413`, the place values produce `8`, `6`, `4`, `1`, and `3`. The last two digits are odd, so the answer is `2`.

```go
func countOddDigit(n int) int {
    value := n
    if value < 0 {
        value = -value
    }

    if value == 0 {
        return 0
    }

    place := 1
    for value/place >= 10 {
        place *= 10
    }

    count := 0
    for place > 0 {
        digit := value / place
        if digit%2 == 1 {
            count++
        }
        value %= place
        place /= 10
    }

    return count
}
```

The first loop finds the highest power of ten in the number. The second loop reads digits from left to right. This avoids recursion, but it needs an extra pass to find the starting place value.

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

A small loop is enough here. Take the last digit with `% 10`, check whether it is odd, and then remove it with `/ 10`. The original number does not need to be saved because the answer only depends on each digit.

For `n = 48271`, the digits checked are `1, 7, 2, 8, 4`. The odd ones are `1` and `7`, so the result is `2`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countOddDigit(int n) {
        long long value = n;
        if (value < 0) {
            value = -value;
        }

        int count = 0;
        while (value > 0) {
            int digit = value % 10;
            if (digit % 2 == 1) {
                count++;
            }
            value /= 10;
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

A value of `0` returns `0`, since zero is even. Using `long long` for the temporary value also avoids trouble with the smallest negative `int`.

Time complexity: `O(d)`, where `d` is the number of digits.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version converts the absolute value to text and checks each character. A digit is odd when its numeric value has a remainder of `1` after division by `2`.

The string method is easy to read, although it uses extra memory for the digit characters. For example, `7314` gives three odd digits: `7`, `3`, and `1`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countOddDigit(int n) {
        long value = n;
        if (value < 0) {
            value = -value;
        }

        String digits = Long.toString(value);
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < digits.length(); i++) {
            int digit = digits.charAt(i) - '0';
            if (digit % 2 == 1) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

The input `0` becomes the one-character string `"0"`, so it is checked correctly and does not increase the count. Negative input is handled by looking at its absolute value.

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(d)` for the string.

The number is turned into a positive value first, then each last digit is tested. The condition `digit % 2 !== 0` tells whether the digit is odd.

There is no need to store the digits. The temporary number gets smaller after every division by `10`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    countOddDigit(n) {
        let value = Math.abs(n);
        let count = 0;

        while (value > 0) {
            const digit = value % 10;
            if (digit % 2 !== 0) {
                count++;
            }
            value = Math.floor(value / 10);
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

For `n = 12040`, the even digits and zero are ignored, leaving only `1`, so the answer is `1`. When the input is `0`, the loop does not run and the method returns `0`.

Time complexity: `O(d)`, with one pass over the digits.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The recursive function removes one digit at every call. It checks the last digit first, then calls itself with the remaining number.

For a dry run with `n = 9052`, the digits seen are `2`, `5`, `0`, and `9`. Only `5` and `9` are odd, so the returned count is `2`.

```python
class Solution:
    def countOddDigit(self, n):
        value = abs(n)

        def count_digits(number):
            if number == 0:
                return 0

            last_digit = number % 10
            current = 1 if last_digit % 2 == 1 else 0
            return current + count_digits(number // 10)

        return count_digits(value)
```

The base case also makes `0` return `0`. This is short, but recursion adds a call for every digit, so it uses more stack space than a loop.

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(d)` because of the recursive calls.

## Intuition
We need each digit fromm the number which can be obtained by using modulo operation. As if any number will be modulo with 10, the reult will always be the last digit of that number. I used this basic maths.

## Approach
1. Initialize a variable to count the frequency of odd digits in the given number (say count).
2.As we need each digit so we start a while loop until value of n becomes 0.
3.Do the modulo of given number n with 10 and store that remainder in a new variable (say r).
4.Check if r is not divided by 2 that means on performing modulo operation on r by 2 if remainder is not 0 then
5.Increment the value of count.
6.Remove the last digit from the number by dividing that number by 10.
7.End the while loop
8.return the count variable

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int countOddDigit(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        while(n>0){
            int r = n % 10;
            if(r%2 != 0) count++;
            n /= 10;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

