## Approach
1. First initialize a count variable to 0, which stores the number of odd digits found. 
2. The while condition checks if the particular number is greater than 0. If yes, it proceeds into the while loop.
3. This is where the core concept of the problem arrives - **Extraction of digits: set a lastDigit variable which extracts the last digit of a number using the modulo operator. A modulo operator is used since it gives the remainder when divided.**
4. Now we are checking if the lastDigit is an odd number. **The condition for checking if a digit is odd number is %2!=0.**
5. If is is true, it increments the count by 1 (count++).
6. Finally it resets is number by dividing by 10
7. The same process repeats until the while condition becomes false.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int countOddDigit(int n) {
        int count=0;
        while(n>0)
        {
            int lastDigit=n%10;
            if(lastDigit%2!=0)
            {
                count++;
            }
            n=n/10;
        }
        return count;

    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(log10(n)) - If n has d digits, the loop runs d times because n is divided by 10 after each iteration. Therefore, the time complexity is O(d), equivalent to O(log10(n)) for positive n.

**Space Complexity:**
O(1) - The solution uses only the variables count and lastDigit, along with the input variable n. The extra space complexity is O(1).

